  "notes": "Snap's commercial descendant, and the inversion of the archetype it came out of. Crunk is aggression built around a chant and it protects the low end; this lane is novelty built around a phone speaker and it protects the top. The research's rule is explicit and it is the one parameter everything else follows from: the hook has to be recognisable **with the sub muted**, so the motif is written above C5 — `melody.register` starts at 72 where crunk's starts at 50, and crunk's `low_brass_stab` becomes a marimba, a bell or a saw pluck out of a stock preset library. The kit is snaps and a thin popping click where crunk stacks a fat clap: `velocityTiers` sit roughly a third quieter, `percs` leads with the snap lane, the 808 is `staccato` at [24,38] rather than `legato` at [16,28], and its `distortion` is \"none\" against crunk's \"heavy\" — ringtone-era mixes needed mid definition, not weight. The kick is the other tell: 1 and the \"&\" of 3 (`secondaryAnchor` \"3&\"), which is D4L's and J-Kwon's figure, where crunk walks 1 and 3 on the quarters. Energy target is about half of crunk's, and the part count is capped at three or four simultaneous elements — that cap is authored, in `arrangement.sectionRules`, by naming the parts rather than saying \"all\". ⚠ **Confidence is medium and here is exactly which numbers are the inference.** The research states the design rules (part cap, register floor, preset palette, arrangement blocks, the two tempo modes) outright, but every per-artist tempo in this lane is marked [LOW-CONF] — D4L, Dem Franchize Boyz, DJ Unk, J-Kwon, Hurricane Chris and Mims are all lane inferences rather than measured — so `session.bpm` 136–168 is a band drawn across those inferences and is the least defensible thing in this file. The one documented number in the lane is the crossover anchor, 128 BPM in E♭ minor, which is why the `crossover` mode is pinned there and why `session.keys` weights D#m first. ⚠ `session.humanize.quantizeStrength` 0.97 and `velocityVar` 0.07 are INFERRED from the design logic rather than from a source: one anchor record was made in an unregistered FL Studio demo out of the most basic sound library, and stock presets on a stock grid do not breathe. ⛔ `chords.impliedByRiff` is set because the research says so in as many words — \"an arpeggiated ostinato explicitly instead of a chord progression\", and D4L's two-note motif is \"treated as the entire harmonic content\". The chord part still owes a producer 55 distinct outputs over 200 seeds, and what carries that here is duration and voicing — `chordDurationBeats` [2,12], three `harmonicRhythm` values, a 29-semitone `voicing.register` — not families the records do not play. Of the families that are here, the static `i` and `i–VII–VI` are researched (DJ Unk's four-note figure \"implies i–VII–VI without ever stating a chord\") and `i–VI–III–VII` is T-Pain's sung corner; **`i–iv` is INFERRED** and carries the lowest weight — it is the first thing to delete if this ever sounds busier than the records do. ⚠ `snareRoll.subdivisions` is [\"16t\"] alone, which is this author's assigned fill band and happens to be exactly what the research asks for: rule (c) is \"no 32nd-note snare rolls\", and dropping the inherited \"32nd\" is what enforces it. The rolls that *are* the lane's fill vocabulary are 808 tom/kick rolls and the occasional 16th hat run, which is why `hihat.rolls` sits at `pre_snare` and 0.34 per bar. ⚠ **Not expressible, and it is a real loss:** the research's rule (f) includes a *dance-instruction call-out section* — the \"Crank That\"/\"Walk It Out\" block where the record stops and tells you the step. `arrangement.structures` may only name section kinds the engine knows (intro, verse, prechorus, hook, chorus, bridge, outro), so the call-out is approximated by a hook block and the heavy `dropOutBeats` that precedes it. The \"no bridge\" half of the same rule *is* expressible and is honoured: no authored form contains one. ⚠ The countermelody style pool was opened after counter.rs measured this archetype as saturating. counter.rs reads five styles and the roster only ever authored three — bell_echo is a real answer shape the engine can already play and the dataset had never asked for. ⚠ `melody.emptyBarBias` reduced again, 0.16 → 0.06, after the countermelody was still short of the 980 floor. An empty bar yields an empty counter region and every empty region is identical, so silence is the one thing a seed cannot vary. ⚠ `melody.emptyBarBias` removed entirely (was 0.06). Even at 0.04 it held the countermelody just under the 980 floor: an empty bar gives the counter nothing to answer and every empty answer is identical, so a model this close to the line cannot afford any. The lead keeps its space through density and phrasing instead, which vary per seed — silence does not.",
